2244 0 obj
<<
/Title (When the number of students is small, manual calculation may seem convenient. However, the same steps must be repeated carefully each time. A subject can be missed while adding marks, a total may be copied incorrectly, or a grade boundary may be applied inconsistently. Even a small arithmetic mistake can make the displayed result incorrect. A computer program can perform the same operations in the same order whenever it is given valid input.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1864 0 R
/Prev 2053 0 R
/Next 2245 0 R
>>
endobj
2245 0 obj
<<
/Title (The need for this project is therefore not simply to replace addition with a computer. It is to demonstrate a consistent process in which input is checked, calculations are performed using a defined formula and decisions follow written rules. The program should also give a clear message when an entry cannot be accepted, rather than quietly using an invalid value.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1864 0 R
/Prev 2244 0 R
/Next 2054 0 R
>>
endobj
2246 0 obj
<<
/Title (The problem addressed by this project is the repetitive calculation of a student's result from marks obtained in several subjects. A correct result requires more than finding a total. The program must also ensure that the entered marks are valid, calculate the percentage, apply the grade thresholds in the correct order and check the passing condition for every subject.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2055 0 R
/Next 2343 0 R
>>
endobj
2247 0 obj
<<
/Title (The required output is a compact result summary showing each subject's marks, the total out of 500, the percentage, the grade and the final status. The purpose is to make the calculation quick, repeatable and easy to verify. The grading thresholds used in this report are project rules for demonstration and should be replaced if an official institutional policy specifies different rules.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2055 0 R
/Prev 2344 0 R
>>
endobj
2248 0 obj
<<
/Title (4. OBJECTIVES OF THE PROJECT)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 2055 0 R
/First 2345 0 R
/Last 2346 0 R
/Count -4
/Next 2347 0 R
>>
endobj
2249 0 obj
<<
/Title (17. ADVANTAGES OF THE SYSTEM)
/Dest [43 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 2348 0 R
/First 2349 0 R
/Last 2350 0 R
/Count -4
/Next 2056 0 R
>>
endobj
2250 0 obj
<<
/Title (The current version has a deliberately limited scope. It calculates the result for one student at a time, uses five fixed subjects and does not preserve the result after the program closes. The user must enter all marks manually. There is no database, student search facility or automatic import of marks from another system.)
/Dest [44 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2056 0 R
/Next 2351 0 R
>>
endobj
2251 0 obj
<<
/Title (A production-quality result system would require additional safeguards, including authentication, permissions, backups, change history and an institution-approved grading policy. These features are not included in the micro-project because its primary purpose is to demonstrate the fundamentals of Python programming.)
/Dest [44 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2056 0 R
/Prev 2352 0 R
>>
endobj
2252 0 obj
<<
/Title (The program can check whether a mark is numeric and within the permitted range, but it cannot know whether the entered value is the official mark awarded by a teacher. The accuracy of the output therefore depends on the accuracy of the input. In a real system, marks may need to be entered by authorised staff and checked against official records.)
/Dest [45 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1865 0 R
/Prev 2057 0 R
/Next 2253 0 R
>>
endobj
2253 0 obj
<<
/Title (If file storage or online access is added, the system should limit who can view or change records. Stored files should be protected from accidental sharing, and backups should be handled securely. It is also useful to keep a record of changes so that mistakes can be traced and corrected.)
/Dest [45 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1865 0 R
/Prev 2252 0 R
/Next 2058 0 R
>>
endobj
2254 0 obj
<<
/Title (This micro-project applies fundamental Python concepts to a practical academic problem. The work involves understanding the requirements, preparing an algorithm, validating input, implementing calculations and testing the output. The project demonstrates that a correct result depends on both accurate arithmetic and correct decision-making. In particular, the program checks the minimum passing mark in each subject before assigning a passing grade.)
/Dest [46 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2060 0 R
/Next 2255 0 R
>>
endobj
2255 0 obj
<<
/Title (The project meets its educational objective by providing a small, understandable application that can be executed in a standard Python environment. Its limitations also provide a clear path for future work, such as support for multiple students, persistent storage and a graphical interface. If the application is ever used beyond a classroom demonstration, the grading rules should be verified and student data should be protected appropriately.)
/Dest [46 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2060 0 R
/Prev 2254 0 R
>>
endobj
2256 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. Python Software Foundation, Built-in Functions: https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html)
/Dest [46 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1867 0 R
/Prev 2061 0 R
/Next 2353 0 R
>>
endobj
