/Title (The application will accept five marks, each with a maximum value of 100. Therefore, the maximum aggregate is 500. A mark must be numeric and must fall between 0 and 100, inclusive. If a user enters letters, a negative number or a number greater than 100, the program should reject the entry and ask for a valid mark.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2055 0 R
/Prev 2246 0 R
/Next 2344 0 R
>>
endobj
2344 0 obj
<<
/Title (The program must apply a minimum passing mark of 33 in every subject. This condition is important because a high total does not necessarily mean that a student has passed all subjects. If even one subject has a mark below 33, the final status must be FAIL. Only after this check should a passing grade be assigned.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2055 0 R
/Prev 2343 0 R
/Next 2247 0 R
>>
endobj
2345 0 obj
<<
/Title (The main objective of the Student Result Calculator is to develop a simple Python program that performs basic result calculations automatically. The project is designed to be small enough for a student to understand, but complete enough to demonstrate how a real task can be converted into a working program.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2248 0 R
/Next 2363 0 R
>>
endobj
2346 0 obj
<<
/Title (Another objective is to demonstrate core Python features in a meaningful context. These include variables, lists, loops, input/output, type conversion, arithmetic expressions, conditional statements and exception handling. The project also aims to produce readable output and code that can be modified later. A future version could accept student details, process a class list or save the result in a file.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2248 0 R
/Prev 2364 0 R
>>
endobj
2347 0 obj
<<
