2349 0 obj
<<
/Title (One major advantage is that the program performs repeated calculations automatically. The user does not need to add five marks manually or calculate the percentage separately. This saves a small amount of time for one result and can save more time if the program is later extended to process multiple students.)
/Dest [43 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2249 0 R
/Next 2371 0 R
>>
endobj
2350 0 obj
<<
/Title (The project is also useful from an educational perspective. It brings together several basic programming ideas in a single application and encourages the student to think about program structure, decision order and test cases. The code is short enough to understand but can be improved in many ways as the learner gains experience.)
/Dest [43 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2249 0 R
/Prev 2372 0 R
>>
endobj
2351 0 obj
<<
/Title (The console interface is suitable for learning but may not be ideal for users who are unfamiliar with command-line programs. The program also assumes that all subjects have the same maximum mark. If a course uses different maximum marks or weighted assessments, the formula and data structure would need to be updated.)
/Dest [44 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2056 0 R
/Prev 2250 0 R
/Next 2352 0 R
>>
endobj
2352 0 obj
<<
/Title (Several enhancements are possible. The program could ask for a student's name and roll number, allow the user to choose subjects, process a list of students and save results in a CSV file. A database could be added for persistent storage, while a graphical interface could make data entry more convenient. Automated unit tests could be written for the grade and pass/fail functions.)
/Dest [44 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2056 0 R
/Prev 2351 0 R
/Next 2251 0 R
>>
endobj
