/Title (The first objective is to collect marks for five clearly named subjects. The program should make it obvious which subject is being requested so that the user does not enter a value in the wrong place. Each value must be checked before it is accepted. Validating the input prevents values outside the permitted range from affecting the result.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2248 0 R
/Prev 2345 0 R
/Next 2364 0 R
>>
endobj
2364 0 obj
<<
/Title (The second objective is to calculate the aggregate and percentage accurately. Since each subject carries 100 marks, the maximum total is 500. The percentage is obtained by dividing the total by the maximum total and multiplying by 100. The program then uses the percentage and subject-wise passing condition to decide the grade and status.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2248 0 R
/Prev 2363 0 R
/Next 2346 0 R
>>
endobj
2365 0 obj
<<
/Title (The current version is a console-based program for one student's result. It uses five fixed subjects and assumes that each subject has a maximum of 100 marks. The program validates the input, calculates the total and percentage, assigns a grade and displays the final status. This limited scope keeps the main logic straightforward and makes it appropriate for a micro-project.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2347 0 R
/Next 2378 0 R
>>
endobj
2366 0 obj
<<
/Title (The project can be extended in several directions. A list of students could be processed in one run, results could be saved to a CSV file, and a database could be used to retrieve student information. A graphical interface could make data entry easier for non-technical users. Before any real deployment, the grading rules and data-handling procedures would need to be checked and approved by the institution.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2347 0 R
/Prev 2379 0 R
>>
endobj
