2369 0 obj
<<
/Title (Testing is an important part of software development because a program that runs without an error is not necessarily a program that produces the correct answer. Testing compares the actual output with the expected output for carefully selected inputs. For the Student Result Calculator, the tests should cover ordinary marks, grade boundaries, pass/fail conditions and invalid entries.)
/Dest [42 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2348 0 R
/Next 2386 0 R
>>
endobj
2370 0 obj
<<
/Title (Invalid-input testing includes text instead of a number, a negative mark and a mark above 100. The expected behaviour is that the program displays a helpful message and asks for a corrected value. A simple test table can record the input, expected result, actual result and whether the test passed. Testing should be repeated after any change to the code.)
/Dest [42 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2348 0 R
/Prev 2387 0 R
>>
endobj
2371 0 obj
<<
/Title (The program applies the same rules to every valid set of marks. Consistency is important because two students with the same marks should receive the same total, percentage and grade. Since the rules are written in code, they can be reviewed and tested rather than relying on memory during each calculation.)
/Dest [43 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2249 0 R
/Prev 2349 0 R
/Next 2372 0 R
>>
endobj
2372 0 obj
<<
/Title (Input validation is another advantage. It prevents a negative mark or a mark greater than 100 from being accepted accidentally. Exception handling also helps the program recover when a user enters text instead of a number. Clear output makes it easier to check the result and explain the calculation during a practical demonstration.)
/Dest [43 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2249 0 R
/Prev 2371 0 R
/Next 2350 0 R
>>
endobj
